So, about the story: It's called "The Minister's Black Veil" and was written by this guy called Hawthorne. but more about him later.

In the story. there's a pastor called Mr. Hooper who one day puts on a weird black veil and it freaks EVERYONE out. What's more is no matter how much people ask, he refuses to take it off. He even refuses to take it off for his wife and that makes her leave him.

The thing I found REALLY COOL about the story though, is how it connects having a face to our humanity. Isn't that INTERESTING?

So basically, the Veil represents a sort of disconnect between Mr. Hooper and everyone else. It told others that he had something to hide.

"But that piece of crape, to their imagination, seemed to hang down before his heart, the symbol of a fearful secret between him and them"

If you take your time to analyze this quote, you'll notice two main things:

1.)
    To the people around Mr. Hooper, he SEEMS like he has a big secret that he's desperate to hide. Of course, the only evidence they have to this is, well, the black veil, which isn't much. Because of this, one can deduce that the people around Mr. Hooper are experiencing an irrational fear of him due to his lack of a face.


    An otherwise normal looking person who has no face is just scary. Like seriously, imagine if you were at McDonalds, or god-forbid Burger King, and the guy taking your order had no face. You wouldn't like that, probably. And yes, the black veil only HIDES Mr. Hooper's face, but It freaks our monkey brains out a ton when we can't see someone's face.

2.)
    The Veil is something BETWEEN Mr. Hooper and the others. It is a dividing wall of secrecy between Mr. Hooper and everyone else.

Personally, I don't blame the Puritans for being so sceptical and distrusting of Mr. Hooper. Suddenly putting on a black veil and wearing it for the rest of your life is a REALLY weird thing to do. Along with that: humans are naturally curious; the people couldn't help but wish to take off the veil, for it is their human nature.

You surely remember Covid and the whole pandemic. The masks that the science belivers correctly wore were constantly and strongly opposed by idiots who would rather drink bleach than perform basic hygiene. One of the main reasons for this stupidity was because a mask appearently provided too large of a hurdle when it came to social interaction for them. This actually makes a lot of sense for the same reasons we've mentioned before. People always have faces. It's not like an arm or a leg, where some people might not have one. No. I can damn near guarantee that you'll NEVER, EVER, meet a human who wholy lacks facial features.

Another way one might observe the importance of a face to the human image is in the Uncanny Valley. The Uncanny Valley refers to how people's acceptance of something changes depending on how human-like it is. A good example of this effect is seen in early 2000's CG.


